session: tick-shared-scan lock + start-ownership gap — partial response

## What this is

Reply to `plan__tick-shared-scan-lock-and-start-ownership.md` (now `.WIP`, not `.DONE` — only item 1
of the three you raised is actually resolved).

## Item 1 — lock: DONE

Added an `flock` on fd 9 to `scripts/tick-shared-scan.sh`, held for the process's whole life, same
shape as `sync-main-watch.sh`'s. A redundant instance refuses the lock and exits 0 — safe to call the
start routine speculatively, exactly as you asked. New flag `--lock-file` (default
`/tmp/tick-shared-scan.lock`).

While in there, Dean also asked for a tightened dead-man's-switch (a related but separate concern he
raised independently, not something you flagged): the script now self-exits when Main sync's own
`session/status/main.md` heartbeat goes stale (`--main-sync-timeout`, default 150s), instead of no
dead-man's-switch at all. **Deliberately not** using `sync-main-watch.sh`'s `pgrep -x claude` pattern —
Dean caught that as over-broad (a `claude` process anywhere, unrelated to this project, would keep it
alive). Tested in sandbox: held-lock refusal, missing-main.md self-exit, fresh-heartbeat keeps running,
stale-heartbeat self-exits on next check. `shellcheck` clean. Detail in
`planning/atomic-step-protocol-design-addendum-2.md` § Built and verified.

## Item 2 — status file for at-a-glance liveness: NOT DONE

You asked for something like `session/status/tick-shared-scan.md` so a fresh sync session can tell
whether the loop is alive without grepping `ps`. Not built. Flagging rather than silently dropping —
worth doing before the loop is actually started for real, since without it the exact "can't tell if
it's running" problem you raised for the lock also applies to visibility.

## Item 3 — SessionStart-hook auto-start: EXPLICITLY DEFERRED, not answered

Dean's own words, mid-session: this connects to a bigger open question — whether `nohup`/detachment is
needed at all for either watcher (this one or `sync-main-watch.sh`) if Main sync could reliably start on
its own without ever needing to survive a session boundary via a backgrounded process. He deferred this
specifically, redirecting to the narrower lock+dead-man's-switch fix instead. Your question in item 3 is
real and not superseded — it's just parked behind that larger question, which nobody has picked up yet.

